.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt NETLINK 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm netlink ,
.Nm rtnetlink
.Nd the kernel's answers and notifications about interfaces, addresses, routes and sockets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In linux/netlink.h
.In linux/rtnetlink.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_NETLINK" "SOCK_RAW" "NETLINK_ROUTE"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Dv AF_NETLINK
socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_ROUTE
(0) is how a program asks the kernel what network interfaces, IPv4 and
IPv6 addresses, routes and neighbours it has, as on Linux.
.Fa type
is
.Dv SOCK_RAW
or
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM ,
which are the same socket, and may carry
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK .
Any user may open one.
.Pp
A socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_GENERIC
(16) discovers families, sends their requests and receives their multicast
events, as
.Xr genetlink 7
specifies.
.Pp
A socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_SOCK_DIAG
(4) answers the dump request
.Dv SOCK_DIAG_BY_FAMILY
(20), with which
.Xr ss 8
lists sockets.
For
.Dv AF_INET
or
.Dv AF_INET6
and the protocol 6, 17 or 255 it gives the TCP, UDP or raw sockets of that
family in the states the request's mask has, each a
.Vt struct inet_diag_msg
with the ports and addresses, the queues, the owner and the inode, a
listener's queues being the connections waiting to be accepted and the most
it holds, and a
raw socket's protocol in an
.Dv INET_DIAG_PROTOCOL
attribute; the states are TCP's numbers, a datagram socket being 1 when
connected and 7 otherwise.
A listener bound to any address has 0.0.0.0 or ::.
An
.Dv AF_INET6
socket's IPv4 peer is given as a mapped address, ::ffff:a.b.c.d, and a
link-local address's interface in
.Fa idiag_if .
For
.Dv AF_UNIX
it gives the local sockets with a descriptor open, each a
.Vt struct unix_diag_msg
with the name, the peer's inode, the two queues and the owner when the
request's
.Fa udiag_show
asks for them.
Another family or protocol is answered with
.Er ENOENT ,
as Linux answers when no module handles it, and a request without
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP
with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A TCP socket's message carries the timer
.Xr ss 8 Ns 's
.Fl o
shows, the retransmission, zero-window probe or TIME_WAIT timer with its
milliseconds left, and when
.Fa idiag_ext
asks for
.Dv INET_DIAG_INFO ,
the first 104 bytes of
.Vt struct tcp_info ,
which
.Fl i
prints: the state, the loss recovery, the options and window scales, the
retransmission timeout, the round trip and its variance in microseconds,
the segment size, the window and the threshold in segments, and the
segments sent again.
A filter's bytecode in the request is not run, and the memory information
and the other extensions are not given.
.Pp
A socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
(15), which udev's monitors open to hear of devices that come and go, is
made, bound and polled like one of
.Dv NETLINK_ROUTE
and hears nothing, since the kernel sends no uevents; what is sent on it
is dropped.
Another protocol is
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT .
.Pp
A request is a netlink message, a
.Vt struct nlmsghdr
and its body, sent to the kernel with
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr sendto 2
or
.Xr sendmsg 2 ;
the address of a
.Xr sendto 2
is not read, since the kernel is the only destination.
Several messages may be sent in one call; they end at one shorter than a
header or longer than the bytes left, and what follows is ignored, as
Linux ignores it, so a request may be sent in a buffer longer than itself,
as iproute2's
.Xr ip 8
sends one.
The answers are queued on the socket as datagrams of messages, each at
most 3776 bytes, or, once the socket has read with a larger buffer, at
most that buffer's size and 32448 bytes, as Linux packs a dump, which
.Xr recv 2
reads one at a time; a receive reports the kernel's address, a
.Vt struct sockaddr_nl
with port id 0.
.Xr recvmsg 2
with
.Dv MSG_PEEK
and
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
gives the length of the datagram waiting and leaves it, which is how
.Xr ip 8
sizes its buffer.
Each answer repeats the request's sequence number and the socket's port
id.
.Pp
A request's family, the first byte of its body, chooses the tables:
.Dv AF_INET
IPv4's,
.Dv AF_INET6
IPv6's, and
.Dv AF_UNSPEC
(0) both, IPv4's first, as
.Ql ip addr
asks and
.Ql ip -6 addr
does not.
.Pp
The requests answered are:
.Bl -tag -width "RTM_GETQDISC"
.It Dv RTM_GETLINK
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK
message for each interface, with its index, its flags
.Dv ( IFF_UP
while it is up,
.Dv IFF_RUNNING
and
.Dv IFF_LOWER_UP
while it is up and its card reports its link up, and
.Dv IFF_BROADCAST
and
.Dv IFF_MULTICAST ) ,
and the attributes
.Dv IFLA_IFNAME ,
.Dv IFLA_ADDRESS ,
.Dv IFLA_BROADCAST ,
.Dv IFLA_MTU ,
.Dv IFLA_OPERSTATE ,
.Dv IFLA_STATS
with the packets, bytes and drops each way and the receive errors, and
.Dv IFLA_STATS64
with the same counters in Linux's 25 eight-byte words, the rest 0, which
.Ql ip -s link
prints.
Without it, the one message of the interface whose index the
.Vt struct ifinfomsg
names, or with an index of 0, whose name an
.Dv IFLA_IFNAME
attribute holds, with or without its terminating NUL, which is how
.Ql ip link show eth0
asks; or an
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
of
.Er ENODEV .
.It Dv RTM_GETADDR
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR
message for each IPv4 address of each interface, the first ones before the
others, which have
.Dv IFA_F_SECONDARY :
its prefix length and
the attributes
.Dv IFA_ADDRESS ,
.Dv IFA_LOCAL ,
.Dv IFA_BROADCAST ,
which a /31 and a /32 have not,
and
.Dv IFA_LABEL ;
and for each IPv6 address, the interface's in the order it took them,
its prefix length, its scope
.Dv ( RT_SCOPE_LINK
for fe80::/10,
.Dv RT_SCOPE_HOST
for ::1), its flags, among them
.Dv IFA_F_PERMANENT ,
.Dv IFA_F_TENTATIVE ,
.Dv IFA_F_DEPRECATED
and
.Dv IFA_F_TEMPORARY ,
and the attributes
.Dv IFA_ADDRESS ,
.Dv IFA_CACHEINFO ,
the preferred and valid seconds left, 0xffffffff for ever, and the
hundredths of a second since boot at which it was made or renewed,
.Dv IFA_FLAGS
and
.Dv IFA_PROTO ,
its maker: 1 for ::1, 2 for a router's prefix, 3 for the link-local
address, none for one made by hand.
.It Dv RTM_GETROUTE
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWROUTE
message for each route of the main table: each interface's subnets but
a /32's, reached on the link, its default route through its gateway when it has
one, and the routes programs added, with
.Dv RTA_PRIORITY
for a metric; and each route of IPv6's table: the prefixes of the
interfaces' addresses at metric 256, the prefixes routers advertise at 256
and the routers themselves as default routes at 1024, with
.Dv RTA_CACHEINFO
giving the hundredths of a second left, and the routes programs added,
each with its
.Dv RTA_TABLE ,
.Dv RTA_OIF ,
.Dv RTA_GATEWAY
for a router, and the protocol that made it: 2 for an address's prefix,
9 for a router's, 3 for one added by
.Xr ioctl 2
or netlink without one.
Without
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
as
.Ql ip route get
asks, one
.Dv RTM_NEWROUTE
message for the address of the request's
.Dv RTA_DST :
a local route in the local table for an address an interface holds, and
otherwise the unicast route the stack takes, with its interface, source
address and gateway, marked
.Dv RTM_F_CLONED
as Linux marks a route it looked up; an address nothing reaches is
.Er ENETUNREACH .
An IPv6 destination, as
.Ql ip -6 route get
asks, is answered the same way: ::1 and the machine's own addresses by a
local route through
.Ql lo ,
another by the card, router and source address a datagram to it would
take, a request's
.Dv RTA_OIF
choosing the card for a link-local one.
.It Dv RTM_GETRULE
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
the three rules every Linux machine starts with, which are what a route
lookup here amounts to: the local table at priority 0, the main table at
32766 and the default table at 32767; and for IPv6 the two of Linux's,
the local table at 0 and the main at 32766.
.It Dv RTM_GETQDISC
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
a
.Ql noqueue
discipline at the root of each interface, since a frame goes to the card
when it is sent, with no queue before it.
.It Dv RTM_GETNETCONF
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP
and the family
.Dv AF_INET
or none, the IPv4 configuration of each interface, then of all, index -1,
and of the default, index -2, as
.Ql ip netconf
prints it: forwarding, the reverse-path filter, multicast forwarding,
proxy ARP and the routes through a link that is down, all off, since the
stack does none of them; with
.Dv AF_INET6
or none, the same for IPv6, without the reverse-path filter, whose
forwarding is
.Va net.ipv6.conf.*.forwarding
in
.Xr proc 5 .
Without it, the configuration its
.Dv NETCONFA_IFINDEX
attribute names, of IPv6 for
.Dv AF_INET6
and of IPv4 otherwise, or
.Er EINVAL
with none and
.Er ENODEV
for an index no interface has.
.It Dv RTM_GETNEIGH
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWNEIGH
message for each mapping of the ARP cache, with the attributes
.Dv NDA_DST
and
.Dv NDA_LLADDR ,
in the state
.Dv NUD_REACHABLE ,
since the cache keeps no age, or
.Dv NUD_PERMANENT
for one set by hand as permanent; only those of one interface when the
.Vt struct ndmsg
or an
.Dv NDA_IFINDEX
attribute names it, and none for the proxy table, which this kernel does
not have.
For
.Dv AF_INET6
or any, each entry of IPv6's neighbour cache, with its
.Dv NDA_LLADDR
once known, in its state of RFC 4861 by Linux's numbers
.Dv ( NUD_INCOMPLETE ,
.Dv NUD_REACHABLE ,
.Dv NUD_STALE ,
.Dv NUD_DELAY ,
.Dv NUD_PROBE ,
.Dv NUD_FAILED
or
.Dv NUD_PERMANENT ) ,
and
.Dv NTF_ROUTER
for a neighbour that said it is a router.
.El
.Pp
A dump ends with an
.Dv NLMSG_DONE
message, and every message of a dump has
.Dv NLM_F_MULTI .
.Pp
A caller with privilege changes the configuration with the requests
.Xr ip 8
sends, which do what the interface requests of
.Xr ioctl 2
do, and are acknowledged with an
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
of 0 when
.Dv NLM_F_ACK
asks for one:
.Bl -tag -width "RTM_DELNEIGH"
.It Dv RTM_NEWLINK , RTM_SETLINK
Take an interface up or down by
.Dv IFF_UP
in the change mask, and set its MTU to
.Dv IFLA_MTU ,
from 68 to 1500 on a card and only 65536 on
.Li lo .
.Dv IFLA_LINKMODE
is a one-byte policy: 0 for the default, 1 for userspace authorization.
A running link in mode 1 starts dormant, and
.Dv IFLA_OPERSTATE
sets it to dormant (5) or up (6), as a supplicant does before and after
authentication.
The two attributes are reported in
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK .
A dormant link keeps
.Dv IFF_LOWER_UP
but clears
.Dv IFF_RUNNING .
Administrative down or lost carrier reports down (2), and a carrier loss
revokes the authorization in mode 1.
Loopback does not accept these policy attributes.
Another MTU or invalid policy value is
.Er EINVAL ,
and an
.Dv IFLA_IFNAME
that would rename the interface
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
either leaves the interface unchanged.
.It Dv RTM_NEWADDR , RTM_DELADDR
Give an interface the address in
.Dv IFA_LOCAL
with the prefix's mask, its first or one more of at most seven, which a
dump reports with
.Dv IFA_F_SECONDARY ,
or take one away; when the first goes, the next takes its place, as
Linux's
.Va promote_secondaries
has it.
For
.Dv AF_INET6 ,
the address in
.Dv IFA_LOCAL
or
.Dv IFA_ADDRESS ,
of any interface,
.Ql lo
among them, one of at most eight: it is tentative until duplicate address
detection passes, unless the request's flags or
.Dv IFA_FLAGS
have
.Dv IFA_F_NODAD ,
lives for the preferred and valid seconds of an
.Dv IFA_CACHEINFO ,
and is permanent without one; its prefix becomes a route of metric 256.
Adding an address it has is
.Er EEXIST
under
.Dv NLM_F_EXCL ,
one past the seven
.Er ENOSPC ,
and removing one it does not have is
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL .
.It Dv RTM_NEWROUTE , RTM_DELROUTE
A default route with no
.Dv RTA_PRIORITY
sets or clears the gateway of the interface
.Dv RTA_OIF
names, or of the one whose subnet has the gateway; adding one where there
is one is
.Er EEXIST
unless
.Dv NLM_F_REPLACE
asks to replace it.
Adding the route an interface's subnet implies is
.Er EEXIST
under
.Dv NLM_F_EXCL .
Any other route goes in the table of 32, by the interface, through
.Dv RTA_GATEWAY
or on the link, of the metric
.Dv RTA_PRIORITY
gives: a gateway none of the interface's subnets has is
.Er ENETUNREACH ,
a route of the same prefix and metric
.Er EEXIST
unless
.Dv NLM_F_REPLACE ,
a destination with bits outside its prefix
.Er EINVAL ,
and a full table
.Er ENOSPC .
Removing a route that is not there is
.Er ESRCH .
A lookup takes the longest prefix, then the lowest metric.
For
.Dv AF_INET6 ,
a route of IPv6's table, of at most 32, by the card
.Dv RTA_OIF
names, else the one the gateway is reached by, else the first, at the
metric of
.Dv RTA_PRIORITY
or 1024: one there already is acknowledged unless
.Dv NLM_F_EXCL
makes it
.Er EEXIST ,
.Dv NLM_F_REPLACE
takes the card's route of the prefix away first,
.Ql lo
is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and a route an address implies cannot be removed.
.It Dv RTM_NEWNEIGH , RTM_DELNEIGH
Store the ARP mapping of
.Dv NDA_DST
to
.Dv NDA_LLADDR
on the interface the
.Vt ndmsg
names, permanent when its state has
.Dv NUD_PERMANENT ,
which no packet changes and no full cache or address change drops; or
forget one, or
.Er ENOENT
when there is none; for
.Dv AF_INET6 ,
the same of IPv6's neighbour cache, a mapping not permanent being
.Dv NUD_STALE
until it is confirmed;
.Ql ip neigh flush
sends one for each.
.El
.Pp
A caller without privilege is refused each with
.Er EPERM .
In a network namespace other than the first, whose one interface is its own
.Ql lo
of index 1, privilege in the user namespace that owns it is enough, a dump
lists that
.Ql lo
alone, and a change is to it alone:
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK
takes it up, giving it 127.0.0.1/8 when it has no address, or down, and
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR
and
.Dv RTM_DELADDR
add and take away 127.0.0.1/8, its only address; any other change is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A request of a type the kernel does not know is answered with an
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
of
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
.Xr bind 2
takes a
.Vt struct sockaddr_nl
of
.Dv AF_NETLINK :
a port id of 0 has the kernel pick one, the process's ID for its first
socket and a number above 2^31 for the rest, and another taken by a
socket of the same protocol already is
.Er EADDRINUSE .
Its
.Fa nl_groups
joins the groups 1 to 32, a bit for each, group N being bit N - 1, and
.Xr setsockopt 2
of level
.Dv SOL_NETLINK
(270) joins one more by its number with
.Dv NETLINK_ADD_MEMBERSHIP
and leaves one with
.Dv NETLINK_DROP_MEMBERSHIP ;
a generic netlink group past 32 is joined by these options; other
protocols accept such a group and send nothing to it.
A group of 0 is
.Er EINVAL .
A socket of
.Dv NETLINK_ROUTE
hears of the changes to the machine's interfaces, addresses and routes as
.Ql ip monitor
shows them on Linux, each a message of its own in a datagram of its own,
with sequence number and port id 0:
.Bl -tag -width "RTNLGRP_IPV4_IFADDR"
.It Dv RTNLGRP_LINK No (1)
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK
when an interface goes up or down, its card's link does, or its MTU,
link mode or operational state changes.
No interface is ever removed, so
.Dv RTM_DELLINK
is never sent.
.It Dv RTNLGRP_IPV4_IFADDR No (5) , Dv RTNLGRP_IPV6_IFADDR No (9)
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR
and
.Dv RTM_DELADDR
when an address comes or goes; an IPv6 address's
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR
again when its state or lifetimes change, as when duplicate detection
ends, it is deprecated or a router renews it.
.It Dv RTNLGRP_IPV4_ROUTE No (7) , Dv RTNLGRP_IPV6_ROUTE No (11)
.Dv RTM_NEWROUTE
and
.Dv RTM_DELROUTE
when a route comes or goes, those an address or a router implies among
them.
.It Dv RTNLGRP_ND_USEROPT No (20)
.Dv RTM_NEWNDUSEROPT ,
68, for each RDNSS (25) and DNSSL (31) option of a router advertisement:
a
.Vt struct nduseroptmsg
with the family
.Dv AF_INET6 ,
the option's length in bytes, the interface's index and the ICMPv6 type
134, then the option as it came, its lifetime and servers or domains
among it, then the router's address in an
.Dv NDUSEROPT_SRCADDR
attribute.
.El
.Pp
The kernel compares the state these are made from with what it was ten
times a second, and after each change a request makes, so a change is
heard within a tenth of a second, and one undone within that time is not
heard.
Neighbours are not announced.
Only sockets of the first network namespace are sent these, and they are
about its interfaces alone: a change to another namespace's
.Ql lo
is not announced.
A socket with 64 KiB of notifications queued misses the next, and its next
receive fails with
.Er ENOBUFS ,
as Linux's does when its buffer is full, unless it set
.Dv NETLINK_NO_ENOBUFS ;
the receives after it go on with what is queued.
Generic netlink also takes
.Dv NETLINK_CAP_ACK
and
.Dv NETLINK_EXT_ACK ,
as
.Xr genetlink 7
specifies.
Another option of the level is accepted and changes nothing.
A socket that sends before it is bound, or whose name
.Xr getsockname 2
asks for, is bound then, as on Linux.
.Xr connect 2
succeeds and changes nothing, and
.Xr getpeername 2
reports the kernel.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr genetlink 7 ,
.Xr ipv6 7
